\section{Near-zero fitted values}{

\code{"multiplicative"} divides by \eqn{\hat{y}}{yhat}, which an SVR does not
constrain to be positive even though the targets are. Where
\eqn{\hat{y}}{yhat} is at or below \code{sqrt(.Machine$double.eps) * mean(abs(y))} - including zero and negative fitted values - the ratio is
inflated, infinite, or sign-flipped.

This function does \strong{not} drop those observations: it always returns
exactly \code{N} values in training-row order, so the result stays aligned
with \code{y_train}, \code{fitted()}, and the training rows. The raw value
(possibly \code{Inf} or \code{NaN}) is returned and a single warning reports how
many observations are affected.

For \emph{pooled} diagnostics (a mean multiplicative error, a variance, a
histogram) the affected observations must be excluded, or one near-zero
fitted value dominates the summary. This follows the standard treatment of
percentage errors near zero (Makridakis): screen the fitted values against a
threshold and drop the observations below it before pooling, rather than
altering the per-observation values. Exclude at the point of aggregation,
e.g.:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{e <- residuals(fit, type = "multiplicative")
mean(e[is.finite(e)])
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}
}

\section{Not reachable through parsnip}{

parsnip registers neither \code{residuals.model_fit} nor \code{fitted.model_fit}, so
calling either generic on a \code{model_fit} dispatches to the stats default and
returns \code{NULL} \strong{silently} - no error, no warning. Reach the psvr object
first with \code{\link[parsnip:reexports]{parsnip::extract_fit_engine()}}, then call \code{residuals()} or
\code{fitted()} on that. psvr deliberately does not register S3 methods on
parsnip's class. Note also that \code{parsnip::augment()} recomputes predictions
on whatever \code{new_data} it is given and reports response residuals only, so
it is not a substitute for the \code{"percentage"} and \code{"multiplicative"} types.
}

